It will be all right because
it's already all right.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:36.873
Right.

00:07:36.873 --> 00:07:37.373
Right.

00:07:37.373 --> 00:07:40.460
It'll be all right, because
it's already all right.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:40.960
Yeah.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:41.919
Happy days.

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:42.879
Happy days.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:43.838
Happy days.

00:07:43.838 --> 00:07:45.756
Happy days.

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:46.716
Whoa.

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:47.383
That's right.

00:07:47.383 --> 00:07:50.887
When you do right, 10
feet tall and a mile high.

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:51.804
Happy days.

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:52.597
Happy days.

00:07:52.597 --> 00:07:53.973
Happy days.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.433
Happy days.

00:07:55.433 --> 00:07:55.933
Whoa.

00:07:55.933 --> 00:07:56.476
That's right.

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.019
When you live right.

00:07:58.019 --> 00:07:59.312
Finally got a piece of the pie.

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:06.444
[END PLAYBACK]

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:08.696
So thank you all,
again, for being here,

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:12.867
and please join me in welcoming
Matthew Cherry and Karen

00:08:12.867 --> 00:08:13.826
Toliver.

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:14.368
Hey, guys.

00:08:14.368 --> 00:08:15.119
How are you doing?

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:15.703
There you are.

00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:17.079
Hello.

00:08:17.079 --> 00:08:17.955
Hey, Brickson.

00:08:17.955 --> 00:08:18.831
Thanks for having us.

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:19.373
Welcome.

00:08:19.373 --> 00:08:21.667
I think you all brought some
friends here too with you.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.586
Can we Say hello to
your little friends?

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.259
Bring that gold man with us.

00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:33.846
Spoiler alert, this
all worked out.

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:36.599
So let's talk about the
journey from beginning

00:08:36.599 --> 00:08:40.144
to those new roommates
that you all have.

00:08:40.144 --> 00:08:40.937
So let's start out.

00:08:40.937 --> 00:08:43.022
We're talking about the
inspiration for the story.

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:44.607
I'd love to begin
with you, Matthew.

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:48.110
Just give us a sense of where
this idea came from and how

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.906
was it brought to life
at the very early stages?

00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:58.079
Ah, man, you know, this journey
started about three years ago,

00:08:58.079 --> 00:08:59.872
actually, four years ago.

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:03.209
I'll take it back to
the earliest point.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:07.630
I remember watching the
Oscars the year of 2016

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:11.133
and trying to make a habit
of watching it every year.

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:13.135
And for this year,
in particular,

00:09:13.135 --> 00:09:15.388
I don't know why I was
paying extra attention

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.933
to all the categories, not
just director, best picture,

00:09:18.933 --> 00:09:23.563
and everything else, watching
documentary short and sound

00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:24.814
editing and sound mixing.

00:09:24.814 --> 00:09:27.858
And I came across the
animated short category,

00:09:27.858 --> 00:09:31.445
and I've had this idea
already in my head.

00:09:31.445 --> 00:09:35.908
And I remember after watching,
I forget who won that year,

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:40.079
watching them win, getting
the same statue that director

00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:41.664
and best picture got.

00:09:41.664 --> 00:09:44.584
And I remember calling my
manager, Monica Young, who

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:46.711
was a producer on the
project too and being like,

00:09:46.711 --> 00:09:49.964
Monica, you know what, I
was like, I got an idea

00:09:49.964 --> 00:09:52.300
for an animated short film.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.634
And I literally showed her this.

00:09:53.634 --> 00:09:55.344
I was like, and if
we do this right,

00:09:55.344 --> 00:09:57.680
I think we could mess
around and win an Oscar.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.141
And she didn't laugh at
me because we had never

00:10:00.141 --> 00:10:05.187
done an animated project before,
and she was very encouraging.

00:10:05.187 --> 00:10:07.356
She was like, all right,
let's try to figure it out.

00:10:07.356 --> 00:10:10.151
And I remember in
2016, I put a tweet

00:10:10.151 --> 00:10:13.821
out saying that I had
an Oscar-worthy animated

00:10:13.821 --> 00:10:15.114
short film idea.

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:17.783
I had come across
this image of--

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:19.910
it was a 3D image
from this artist

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.289
in Brazil named Julian
Santiago, and it

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:28.461
was an Afro-Latino father.

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:31.213
He was sitting at his
workstation trying to draw,

00:10:31.213 --> 00:10:34.467
and his daughter was sitting
on his lap looking up at him

00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:38.888
and just kind of an initial
inspiration in terms of imagery

00:10:38.888 --> 00:10:39.889
for the film.

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:41.766
And I put the tweet
out, trying to see

00:10:41.766 --> 00:10:43.643
if I can get some 3D
artists to holler at me,

00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:47.313
and it kind of fell on
deaf ears a little bit,

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:48.397
for whatever reason.

00:10:48.397 --> 00:10:50.316
And I kind of
forgot out about it

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:53.235
and ended up doing a movie
that I shot on iPhones called

00:10:53.235 --> 00:10:57.615
9 Rides that was shot
in the course of a week

00:10:57.615 --> 00:10:59.909
around the week of
Thanksgiving 2016,

00:10:59.909 --> 00:11:02.703
and then we ended up getting
into South by Southwest.

00:11:02.703 --> 00:11:06.290
And that project always kind
of was in the back of my mind,

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.000
and I was trying
to figure it out,

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.506
and I came across this
artist, Vashti Harrison, who

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:16.759
a friend of mine, a
filmmaker named Nisla Muman,

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:20.930
had posted a picture that she
drew of her for a birthday

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:23.307
around the summer of 2017.

00:11:23.307 --> 00:11:26.811
And I had never seen somebody
draw Black hair like this.

00:11:26.811 --> 00:11:28.521
It was very accurate.

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:32.692
Nisla has incredible braids
and natural hairstyles.

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:35.569
And I reached out to Vashti
and I was like, look,

00:11:35.569 --> 00:11:37.154
I've been thinking
about this project

00:11:37.154 --> 00:11:38.739
I can't get out of my mind.

00:11:38.739 --> 00:11:42.284
I'm thinking about doing
an animated short film,

00:11:42.284 --> 00:11:44.578
and I would love to
see if you would be

00:11:44.578 --> 00:11:46.539
down to do some artwork for us.

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:50.126
And she was down, had
talked to Monica about it.

00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:54.630
We did the Kickstarter campaign,
and the rest was history.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:58.384
And if you move forward
on the next slide,

00:11:58.384 --> 00:12:02.054
a lot of amazing things
happened from it.

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:06.142
We offered different
gifts like a picture book,

00:12:06.142 --> 00:12:08.978
which ended up becoming a
New York Times best seller.

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:11.480
We offered different gifts
like pens and T-shirts

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.023
and all kinds of things.

00:12:13.023 --> 00:12:16.777
And if you go to
the next slide, we

00:12:16.777 --> 00:12:21.282
ended up just having our
initial goal was $75,000,

00:12:21.282 --> 00:12:25.161
and we ended up setting this
record of almost $300,000

00:12:25.161 --> 00:12:27.788
that we raised in the
course of 30 days.

00:12:27.788 --> 00:12:32.918
And till this day, it's still
the highest grossing short film

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:35.087
Kickstarter in the
history of the platform.

00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:38.132
And we just had so many great
people back the project,

00:12:38.132 --> 00:12:40.050
people like Jordan
Peele and Chris Rock

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.596
and Chance the Rapper, Chelsea
Clinton, Dwyane Wade, Gabrielle

00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:46.891
Union, so many great people.

00:12:46.891 --> 00:12:50.019
And it was just crazy
because I remember

00:12:50.019 --> 00:12:51.896
we had a list of all
the different press

00:12:51.896 --> 00:12:55.900
outlets that we wanted to
cover the Kickstarter campaign.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.403
And oftentimes, when
you're doing a Kickstarter,

00:12:59.403 --> 00:13:02.490
it's all pretty
much a pipe dream

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.576
in terms of, hopefully, these
people will respond to you.

00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:07.703
And within the first
week of the project,

00:13:07.703 --> 00:13:10.414
every single outlet that
we had on our target

00:13:10.414 --> 00:13:12.958
have reached out to us
before we even had a chance

00:13:12.958 --> 00:13:13.876
to reach out to them.

00:13:13.876 --> 00:13:16.295
So it was just magic
on this project

00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:17.379
ever since the beginning.

00:13:17.379 --> 00:13:22.426
And for us, I think it was the
combination of being inspired

00:13:22.426 --> 00:13:25.262
by a lot of these viral videos
of the dads that were doing

00:13:25.262 --> 00:13:26.680
their daughter's
hair, and we knew

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.891
that we could capture
that same magic

00:13:28.891 --> 00:13:32.102
that we would be successful
in terms of raising the money.

00:13:32.102 --> 00:13:36.106
And I just think, also, we
dropped this Kickstarter

00:13:36.106 --> 00:13:39.819
at a time where it was right
before this whole inclusion

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.363
and diversity push that
has happened recently

00:13:42.363 --> 00:13:43.656
in the world of animation.

00:13:43.656 --> 00:13:45.407
This is before Moana came out.

00:13:45.407 --> 00:13:46.659
This is before Coco.

00:13:46.659 --> 00:13:48.702
This is before Into
the Spider-Verse.

00:13:48.702 --> 00:13:51.121
And people were
really just clamoring

00:13:51.121 --> 00:13:53.791
to see themselves
represented in this medium

00:13:53.791 --> 00:13:55.668
that Karen said
in their speeches

00:13:55.668 --> 00:13:59.338
the first form of filmmaking
that were exposed to.

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:02.800
And when you don't see yourself,
especially when you're young,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.927
it really does a number
on your self-confidence.

00:14:04.927 --> 00:14:07.137
And I just think it was one
of those situations where

00:14:07.137 --> 00:14:09.265
we were kind of right
place, right time.

00:14:09.265 --> 00:14:13.143
Like Matthew said, I mean, when
I became aware of the project,

00:14:13.143 --> 00:14:15.604
it was before all of this
things, before Moana and Coco,

00:14:15.604 --> 00:14:16.313
and those things.

00:14:16.313 --> 00:14:20.109
And I had been working in
animation as an executive

00:14:20.109 --> 00:14:24.405
first at Disney Pictures, then
I was at Fox for 10 years.

00:14:24.405 --> 00:14:27.199
I worked on the Ice Age
franchise, I worked on Rio,

00:14:27.199 --> 00:14:30.536
and then I was actually
just at the end of Fox

00:14:30.536 --> 00:14:34.623
and coming to Sony when
I knew Monica Young, who

00:14:34.623 --> 00:14:39.003
was one of the producers and
Matthew's manager, socially.

00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:41.213
But we didn't even know,
really, what each other did.

00:14:41.213 --> 00:14:43.549
So all this lead up,
as Matthew got the idea

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:44.925
and did the
Kickstarter, and they

00:14:44.925 --> 00:14:47.136
were looking for
someone to help them

00:14:47.136 --> 00:14:48.387
through the animation process.

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:51.307
And I was lucky enough
that I was one of the few.

00:14:51.307 --> 00:14:55.895
Monica asked if I could
help these guys produce it.

00:14:55.895 --> 00:14:58.272
And having never
in my career been

00:14:58.272 --> 00:15:01.609
able to work on a project
about Black people

00:15:01.609 --> 00:15:05.070
that's so personal,
there's no way to say no.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.488
You just know how
important it is.

00:15:06.488 --> 00:15:09.450
You know how personal it would
be and how fun it would be.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:10.951
But I had huge
caveats because I was

00:15:10.951 --> 00:15:13.037
like I'd never, ever worked
on anything like this

00:15:13.037 --> 00:15:14.288
on this level.

00:15:14.288 --> 00:15:18.959
The movies that I work on are
big, million-dollar movies,

00:15:18.959 --> 00:15:20.377
and there's a whole
infrastructure

00:15:20.377 --> 00:15:23.505
of how these movies get made and
technical people and all that.

00:15:23.505 --> 00:15:27.509
And my expertise is in
the front end of story

00:15:27.509 --> 00:15:28.969
and overseeing the
story and really

00:15:28.969 --> 00:15:30.596
kind of helping put
the pieces together

00:15:30.596 --> 00:15:31.931
and putting the team together.

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:35.100
So when asked, and again, they
raised an incredible amount

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.269
of money on Kickstarter,
but it was still

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:40.981
small to get something to the
caliber that Matthew wanted.

00:15:40.981 --> 00:15:43.859
My biggest caveat
was like, just know

00:15:43.859 --> 00:15:46.487
I have absolutely no
idea how to do this.

00:15:46.487 --> 00:15:47.529
I know what your goal is.

00:15:47.529 --> 00:15:49.740
I know what your intent-- and
his intent was everything.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.784
It was really what led us
all through every stage

00:15:51.784 --> 00:15:54.078
of the process because we
knew he wanted that gold.

00:15:54.078 --> 00:15:54.912
We wanted that gold.

00:15:54.912 --> 00:15:57.122
We were going to figure
it out, but really

00:15:57.122 --> 00:16:00.834
doing it at a small
scale with those means

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:03.837
are more difficult
than you would think.

00:16:03.837 --> 00:16:05.756
And like the image
that's here, we

00:16:05.756 --> 00:16:07.299
knew that we were
going to be telling

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:09.635
a story about a black
family in a short,

00:16:09.635 --> 00:16:11.136
and that had never
really been done.

00:16:11.136 --> 00:16:15.099
And so the goal in holding
the responsibility of that

00:16:15.099 --> 00:16:16.976
was really high on our
minds, because we knew

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:18.227
that they had to be beautiful.

00:16:18.227 --> 00:16:19.478
We knew we had to look great.

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:21.188
In the first couple
of choices, I

00:16:21.188 --> 00:16:22.606
think if you go
to the next slide,

00:16:22.606 --> 00:16:24.525
we knew we were
dealing with hair.

00:16:24.525 --> 00:16:27.695
And again, Vashti's
image was so beautiful

00:16:27.695 --> 00:16:29.989
that we had to make a choice
pretty early on whether we

00:16:29.989 --> 00:16:31.865
were going to do CG or 2D.

00:16:31.865 --> 00:16:34.618
And we knew that it was going to
be very difficult on the budget

00:16:34.618 --> 00:16:37.871
that we had to really do the
loving care that we needed

00:16:37.871 --> 00:16:40.124
for this family and
represent hair and make it

00:16:40.124 --> 00:16:44.545
really a personality and retain
the beauty of Vashti's design.

00:16:44.545 --> 00:16:47.297
So pretty quickly on, we
knew that 2D was really

00:16:47.297 --> 00:16:50.467
going to be the way to go.

00:16:50.467 --> 00:16:51.844
And we just had
to figure out who

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:53.721
was going to be able to
do it because it just

00:16:53.721 --> 00:16:54.680
had to look great.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:56.265
Let's talk about the
character design.

00:16:56.265 --> 00:16:58.851
So we're getting into the
deep heart of animation

00:16:58.851 --> 00:17:02.646
now, so everybody
hold on to your hats.

00:17:02.646 --> 00:17:04.314
Let's talk about
these characters

00:17:04.314 --> 00:17:07.985
and the process of bringing
them to life for the short.

00:17:07.985 --> 00:17:11.071
Yeah, let me set the stage,
and then I'll pass it to Karen.

00:17:11.071 --> 00:17:13.699
So when we did our initial
Kickstarter campaign,

00:17:13.699 --> 00:17:15.367
the dad actually
looked way different

00:17:15.367 --> 00:17:17.619
than he looks in
our final design.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.204
He was a little bigger.

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:22.082
We described him
as a gentle giant.

00:17:22.082 --> 00:17:23.792
He had locks, but
he looked like he

00:17:23.792 --> 00:17:26.837
may have been in his mid 40s.

00:17:26.837 --> 00:17:30.340
I'll say he looked a little
"safer," quote, unquote.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:35.471
And when Karen came on board,
I think one of the best ideas

00:17:35.471 --> 00:17:38.974
that she had was the idea
of just really pushing

00:17:38.974 --> 00:17:43.145
the imagery of how
we perceive Steven.

00:17:43.145 --> 00:17:45.731
It really felt like
an opportunity for us

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:49.026
to normalize young Black
fathers because that's

00:17:49.026 --> 00:17:52.529
a very specific segment
of mainstream media, where

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:54.990
young Black men
are often vilified,

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:59.078
and you don't really see them
in these more softer, nurturing

00:17:59.078 --> 00:17:59.620
roles.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.623
And studies have shown
that Black men are

00:18:02.623 --> 00:18:05.501
amongst the biggest
and most involved

00:18:05.501 --> 00:18:06.960
when it comes to
their kids' lives,

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.338
whether they're with
the mother or not.

00:18:09.338 --> 00:18:12.633
And that was one of
Karen's biggest things

00:18:12.633 --> 00:18:14.218
I felt like she
brought to the project

00:18:14.218 --> 00:18:16.386
because just, really, I
think for a lot of people,

00:18:16.386 --> 00:18:19.014
it just made it even more
relatable and grounded

00:18:19.014 --> 00:18:22.684
and just really represents what
the modern family looks like.

00:18:22.684 --> 00:18:26.438
It was not families being
40 plus and the house

00:18:26.438 --> 00:18:27.606
and the white picket fence.

00:18:27.606 --> 00:18:29.650
It's a little less linear.

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:34.113
And so definitely, I just wanted
to give major props to Karen

00:18:34.113 --> 00:18:37.116
for bringing that idea because
I think that was the thing that

00:18:37.116 --> 00:18:39.451
really opened it up.

00:18:39.451 --> 00:18:43.038
So yeah, the challenge
with animation

00:18:43.038 --> 00:18:45.916
is every level of
detail just makes

00:18:45.916 --> 00:18:48.752
it more difficult to
animate, and so we

00:18:48.752 --> 00:18:51.171
had the challenge of taking
Vashti's drawings, which

00:18:51.171 --> 00:18:54.508
is so beautiful and very
delicate and detailed

00:18:54.508 --> 00:18:55.425
in the book.

00:18:55.425 --> 00:18:59.513
And as Matthew said, everything
about this process with Hair

00:18:59.513 --> 00:19:00.973
Love was unconventional.

00:19:00.973 --> 00:19:02.516
So that was another
thing I learned.

00:19:02.516 --> 00:19:03.892
Don't worry about
what you've been told.

00:19:03.892 --> 00:19:06.228
Usually, you either get a
book and then you make a short

00:19:06.228 --> 00:19:07.062
or maybe you make--

00:19:07.062 --> 00:19:09.439
I guess people don't even make
a short and then make a book.

00:19:09.439 --> 00:19:11.567
So the fact that this was
going on at the same time

00:19:11.567 --> 00:19:13.735
is highly unusual
because usually, one

00:19:13.735 --> 00:19:14.778
would drive the other.

00:19:14.778 --> 00:19:16.905
So we knew that we
wanted Vashti's designs

00:19:16.905 --> 00:19:19.783
to be really important,
but these designs,

00:19:19.783 --> 00:19:23.495
from a technical standpoint,
felt too detailed

00:19:23.495 --> 00:19:26.415
to really allow the animators
to do their thing, particularly

00:19:26.415 --> 00:19:27.583
with the budget that we had.

00:19:27.583 --> 00:19:29.626
So part of our journey
was to figure out

00:19:29.626 --> 00:19:32.796
how to make sure that you could
identify Zuri as the book went

00:19:32.796 --> 00:19:35.257
along and people were going to
get to know her in the book,

00:19:35.257 --> 00:19:37.259
but make sure that she
was representative enough

00:19:37.259 --> 00:19:39.803
in the short so that she
felt like the same character.

00:19:39.803 --> 00:19:42.556
But at the same time,
we were using all sorts

00:19:42.556 --> 00:19:46.059
of ideas of how graphic we
wanted the character to be

00:19:46.059 --> 00:19:48.812
in the short and had to figure
out how to translate that.

00:19:48.812 --> 00:19:50.689
So that was really
important, and it

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:53.984
was a lot of technical things
that we had to figure out

00:19:53.984 --> 00:19:54.735
to make that work.

00:19:54.735 --> 00:19:57.779
But again, our team was
amazing, and a lot of it

00:19:57.779 --> 00:20:01.283
was about hair and the
software that we were using.

00:20:01.283 --> 00:20:04.036
So there's a process
called Harmony,

00:20:04.036 --> 00:20:07.206
and that's a 2D
software, but we had

00:20:07.206 --> 00:20:08.999
to figure out how to
translate in this,

00:20:08.999 --> 00:20:10.626
and we'll talk about
that a little bit.

00:20:10.626 --> 00:20:13.170
And so this is
the first designs,

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:14.630
and then we can
keep going forward,

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.799
and you can see more of how
she evolved to the process.

00:20:17.799 --> 00:20:22.554
And Vashti did, again,
these expression sheets,

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:24.181
and we fell in love,
and that's what

00:20:24.181 --> 00:20:26.141
helped to get the money
for the Kickstarter

00:20:26.141 --> 00:20:28.352
because people felt like
they already knew this girl.

00:20:28.352 --> 00:20:30.520
So we had to really protect
Zuri through the process

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:32.397
so that we didn't lose her.

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:35.525
And if you go to the next one.

00:20:35.525 --> 00:20:39.655
Then we started looking at
how to simplify that design

00:20:39.655 --> 00:20:40.864
and use other references.

00:20:40.864 --> 00:20:42.866
So here's a series
of picture books

00:20:42.866 --> 00:20:46.787
that had a much more graphic
look and a simplified look.

00:20:46.787 --> 00:20:51.166
So the exercise was how much
could you scale down images

00:20:51.166 --> 00:20:53.126
and still get the connectivity.

00:20:53.126 --> 00:20:56.088
So we love the beauty and
the elegance and simplicity

00:20:56.088 --> 00:20:59.508
of this book, Island Born,
and that was a big inspiration

00:20:59.508 --> 00:21:01.426
as we moved forward.

00:21:01.426 --> 00:21:06.431
There was Ezra Keats, Snowy Day.

00:21:06.431 --> 00:21:08.058
There's a lot of
just beautiful stuff,

00:21:08.058 --> 00:21:10.477
and then Tiffany Ford
was a character designer

00:21:10.477 --> 00:21:13.063
that we fell in love
with and actually, wanted

00:21:13.063 --> 00:21:14.231
to work with on the short.

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:17.985
But we really were
exploring the use of color

00:21:17.985 --> 00:21:21.363
and how that really translated
into the characters.

00:21:21.363 --> 00:21:25.075
And then there's many
others that finally led us

00:21:25.075 --> 00:21:29.621
to the design you see with
Zuri, I think, in the next,

00:21:29.621 --> 00:21:31.039
and this is what we landed on.

00:21:31.039 --> 00:21:35.002
So that was the journey of the
book, the illustrated version

00:21:35.002 --> 00:21:38.922
of Zuri to the one that
we found in animation.

00:21:38.922 --> 00:21:41.383
The other key, and we'll
talk about this in a minute,

00:21:41.383 --> 00:21:44.720
but the contrast between how we
wanted the characters to look,

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.847
which, again, had more
detail, but we really

00:21:46.847 --> 00:21:51.018
wanted to keep an
artistic appeal to each

00:21:51.018 --> 00:21:53.520
of the frames of the thing
so it was really as beautiful

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:54.771
and painterly as possible.

00:21:54.771 --> 00:21:57.190
So you'll notice when we
get to the backgrounds,

00:21:57.190 --> 00:21:58.317
they're not as detailed.

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:00.193
They're much more
watercolor and sort

00:22:00.193 --> 00:22:06.116
of expression kind of vibe,
more than the characters.

00:22:06.116 --> 00:22:09.953
Yeah, and as you can
see, this is probably

00:22:09.953 --> 00:22:12.164
the first iteration of Zuri.

00:22:12.164 --> 00:22:15.625
And while, obviously,
she retains

00:22:15.625 --> 00:22:21.089
some of the charm from Vashti's
initial drawing, for me,

00:22:21.089 --> 00:22:23.133
I was like, we can always
do a little bit better.

00:22:23.133 --> 00:22:27.304
And you can see how when
Bruce Smith came on board

00:22:27.304 --> 00:22:30.182
and was able to really get
in there and into the details

00:22:30.182 --> 00:22:35.020
and how small things like
slightly tweaking the profile,

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:37.939
adjusting the ear
location, having the belly

00:22:37.939 --> 00:22:42.152
protrude a little bit, smaller
feet, so many small details

00:22:42.152 --> 00:22:45.947
that an artist with as
much experience as Bruce,

00:22:45.947 --> 00:22:47.949
he just totally
brings to the table.

00:22:47.949 --> 00:22:49.743
It just makes her
so much more cuter

00:22:49.743 --> 00:22:52.120
and have so much more appeal.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.414
And if you go to the
next slide, these

00:22:54.414 --> 00:22:56.208
are also some different
things that we

00:22:56.208 --> 00:23:00.837
did to try to make her
as cute as possible,

00:23:00.837 --> 00:23:03.256
and this ended up
being our final design.

00:23:03.256 --> 00:23:05.050
And a lot of it was
just a lot of tweaking,

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:09.429
making sure that her skin
color was a certain way, making

00:23:09.429 --> 00:23:13.517
sure the hair texture looked a
very certain way because as you

00:23:13.517 --> 00:23:17.020
guys know in mainstream media,
so often, certain images are

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:19.064
pushed to the forefront,
especially when it comes

00:23:19.064 --> 00:23:20.357
to Black women.

00:23:20.357 --> 00:23:22.025
And for us, it
was very important

00:23:22.025 --> 00:23:26.238
that her hair was as
kinky as we could make it.

00:23:26.238 --> 00:23:28.824
She was brown, more
browner skin tone,

00:23:28.824 --> 00:23:30.492
things like making
the mom a little

00:23:30.492 --> 00:23:32.536
darker than her and the father.

00:23:32.536 --> 00:23:35.497
Just small things that
I think, on the surface,

00:23:35.497 --> 00:23:37.749
may not seem like a
big deal, but were

00:23:37.749 --> 00:23:40.085
things that once the
short film was released,

00:23:40.085 --> 00:23:41.711
they were things
that we constantly

00:23:41.711 --> 00:23:44.631
were getting comments on
about things that they loved,

00:23:44.631 --> 00:23:46.425
especially the hair texture.

00:23:46.425 --> 00:23:50.053
These are some of the early
hairstyle designs that we did.

00:23:50.053 --> 00:23:53.473
As you know, Black women,
their hair is a super power,

00:23:53.473 --> 00:23:56.101
and you can style it
in many different ways,

00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:59.104
and we just wanted to see what
our different hairstyles would

00:23:59.104 --> 00:24:01.940
look on Zuri's cute face.

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:04.776
So these were some
of the hairstyles

00:24:04.776 --> 00:24:07.696
that we did, and you
go to the next one.

00:24:07.696 --> 00:24:09.406
And of course, in
animation, I would just

00:24:09.406 --> 00:24:12.117
say that every time
you change a hairstyle,

00:24:12.117 --> 00:24:14.703
you're changing the rig,
you're doing a lot to it.

00:24:14.703 --> 00:24:17.205
So this is where six point
shows how they were actually

00:24:17.205 --> 00:24:19.749
able to do the hair in sections
to really kind of give it

00:24:19.749 --> 00:24:21.042
the volume that it wanted.

00:24:21.042 --> 00:24:22.961
I think if you hit Play,
it should turn around.

00:24:22.961 --> 00:24:26.965
You can see a little bit
of what that looks like.

00:24:26.965 --> 00:24:27.466
Yeah.

00:24:27.466 --> 00:24:31.428
And so this was our model that
we used in the short film.

00:24:31.428 --> 00:24:35.432
As you can see, the hair
is in different sections.

00:24:35.432 --> 00:24:38.935
And if you go to the next
one, these are literally just

00:24:38.935 --> 00:24:43.356
like a whole sheet of just
all the different hairstyles

00:24:43.356 --> 00:24:46.693
that we had on
mom's YouTube page.

00:24:46.693 --> 00:24:50.989
It's really trying to represent
the full breadth of Black hair,

00:24:50.989 --> 00:24:55.744
and the blog was a really great
opportunity to showcase that.

00:24:55.744 --> 00:24:58.038
Let's go forward, a slide.

00:24:58.038 --> 00:25:00.123
I think we're going to
move into look development.

00:25:00.123 --> 00:25:01.541
Karen, you talked
about this a bit

00:25:01.541 --> 00:25:04.419
before, if we go a little bit
further in terms of the palette

00:25:04.419 --> 00:25:07.005
and the painterly
vision of the film.

00:25:07.005 --> 00:25:11.218
So give us a bit more
and sort of sharpen

00:25:11.218 --> 00:25:14.179
this view and the less
sharp view of the scenery.

00:25:14.179 --> 00:25:15.388
Sure.

00:25:15.388 --> 00:25:16.515
Yeah.

00:25:16.515 --> 00:25:18.725
Again, we really
had high intentions

00:25:18.725 --> 00:25:20.519
about how beautiful we
wanted this to look,

00:25:20.519 --> 00:25:23.897
and we had reference in shorts
like there was a short called

00:25:23.897 --> 00:25:26.483
Dam Keeper that was
very inspirational to us

00:25:26.483 --> 00:25:29.236
because it did have a beautiful
watercolor kind of aesthetic.

00:25:29.236 --> 00:25:33.031
And so again, knowing that we
had a path with the character

00:25:33.031 --> 00:25:34.824
designs because of
the book and Vashti,

00:25:34.824 --> 00:25:37.452
we thought the backgrounds were
really where we could go crazy.

00:25:37.452 --> 00:25:41.957
And so the first thing we do--
and again, I'm not an artist.

00:25:41.957 --> 00:25:44.793
We really leaned on Stacey
Newton and Everett Downing

00:25:44.793 --> 00:25:48.046
to really help us dig deep
on this and pull reference.

00:25:48.046 --> 00:25:52.467
And the first go to for any
animator is Maurice Noble, who

00:25:52.467 --> 00:25:54.970
did a bunch of the Looney Tunes,
and there's just something

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:56.972
really gorgeous about his work.

00:25:56.972 --> 00:25:58.014
And it's very simple.

00:25:58.014 --> 00:26:02.477
You've got the broad colors
and the simple pencil lines.

00:26:02.477 --> 00:26:06.523
And we really were
inspired by that look.

00:26:06.523 --> 00:26:08.650
If you go forward, you
can see the coloring

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:10.944
and just so much that even
without any characters

00:26:10.944 --> 00:26:14.489
in there, the background
themselves stood apart,

00:26:14.489 --> 00:26:16.324
and that was really
the inspiration.

00:26:16.324 --> 00:26:19.786
We wanted to use color
in a really bold way.

00:26:19.786 --> 00:26:21.746
We just found these
really gorgeous.

00:26:21.746 --> 00:26:24.708
And then there was another
artist, Walter Peregoy,

00:26:24.708 --> 00:26:26.710
who worked One Hundred
and One Dalmatians,

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:31.214
so this was incredible,
again, just very stylized,

00:26:31.214 --> 00:26:32.549
but really got the idea across.

00:26:32.549 --> 00:26:35.010
You don't want the background
to really stand out too much.

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:37.220
You really want to support
the foreground, of course,

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.974
but we thought that these
designs were just amazing.

00:26:40.974 --> 00:26:42.767
And there was one
other, Helen Lundberg,

00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:45.645
who's a surrealist painter
they introduced us to.

00:26:45.645 --> 00:26:47.772
So all of this
was the ammunition

00:26:47.772 --> 00:26:53.737
to put just what this
inspiration for how

00:26:53.737 --> 00:26:55.780
we wanted these to look.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:57.741
And so that was the
question is, can you really

00:26:57.741 --> 00:26:59.784
reduce this to color and shape?

00:26:59.784 --> 00:27:01.036
How much can we get away with.

00:27:01.036 --> 00:27:04.372
And this image that you'll
see is Zuri in the background.

00:27:04.372 --> 00:27:07.792
I think our art designer,
Maria Yee, who's amazing

00:27:07.792 --> 00:27:09.586
and when she really
kind of synthesized

00:27:09.586 --> 00:27:13.798
this look in the bathroom,
we realized this was it.

00:27:13.798 --> 00:27:17.093
This really gave us the
look that we wanted.

00:27:17.093 --> 00:27:20.055
We loved how rich and
beautiful the background was.

00:27:20.055 --> 00:27:22.891
We liked how it let Zuri popped
in the front, and we were like,

00:27:22.891 --> 00:27:25.769
OK, every shot of
this short should

00:27:25.769 --> 00:27:28.355
feel like a painting like this,
and if we can get that across,

00:27:28.355 --> 00:27:29.522
I think we're in good shape.

00:27:29.522 --> 00:27:32.859
And so it was really about broad
brush work and just really kind

00:27:32.859 --> 00:27:35.528
of getting that painterly look.

00:27:35.528 --> 00:27:37.947
And so that's really
the first thing we did.

00:27:37.947 --> 00:27:40.742
And then we went on to the
other locations you'll see.

00:27:40.742 --> 00:27:42.243
And the funny thing,
again, in terms

00:27:42.243 --> 00:27:44.079
of the amount of work
we did is that there's

00:27:44.079 --> 00:27:46.956
one location in here you never
see in the short, the living

00:27:46.956 --> 00:27:50.210
room on the bottom right,
which was sad because it

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:51.294
was one of our favorite.

00:27:51.294 --> 00:27:54.547
And as we were designing, we're
like we're on World Market

00:27:54.547 --> 00:27:56.049
picking furniture
and really kind

00:27:56.049 --> 00:27:57.467
of really giving love to this.

00:27:57.467 --> 00:27:59.511
And again, Matt, you
can speak to this,

00:27:59.511 --> 00:28:01.012
but it was, again,
very grounded.

00:28:01.012 --> 00:28:02.013
We know where they live.

00:28:02.013 --> 00:28:04.557
We know what kind of building
it was, and all of that

00:28:04.557 --> 00:28:06.601
was really important.

00:28:06.601 --> 00:28:12.524
Yeah, we spoke about this family
being from Chicago, very much

00:28:12.524 --> 00:28:16.945
not quite middle class,
but working their way up,

00:28:16.945 --> 00:28:20.657
which is so much the experience
of a lot of young 20-year-olds

00:28:20.657 --> 00:28:22.409
that are either
fresh out of college

00:28:22.409 --> 00:28:24.369
or are going into
their late 20s.

00:28:24.369 --> 00:28:27.497
And we wanted the
apartment and brownstone

00:28:27.497 --> 00:28:29.708
that they lived in to
really reflect that.

00:28:29.708 --> 00:28:31.668
And so when we go
to the next slide,

00:28:31.668 --> 00:28:34.421
you'll see an early model
of the bathroom, which

00:28:34.421 --> 00:28:36.923
we knew was going to play such
a big part of the short film.

00:28:36.923 --> 00:28:39.718
Because Zuri was going to be
in there doing her hair styles

00:28:39.718 --> 00:28:41.261
and also, would be
the location when

00:28:41.261 --> 00:28:44.139
Dad stepped up
and was able to do

00:28:44.139 --> 00:28:45.849
her hairstyle the correct way.

00:28:45.849 --> 00:28:49.227
And so this early design
was done in sketch up,

00:28:49.227 --> 00:28:52.522
and then you see the final
design, how beautiful that is.

00:28:52.522 --> 00:28:54.190
I mean, every time
I see this, I'm

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:57.193
just like that, literally,
looks like the bathroom

00:28:57.193 --> 00:29:00.029
that I grew up in in Chicago,
with old-school radiator

00:29:00.029 --> 00:29:04.451
and the tiles and the brick
and even the little drawing

00:29:04.451 --> 00:29:10.623
above the toilet, which
just adds so much charm.

00:29:10.623 --> 00:29:13.334
And so and a lot of the
stuff really evolved as well

00:29:13.334 --> 00:29:14.627
if you go to the next one.

00:29:14.627 --> 00:29:18.506
And you'll see this is an early
version and iteration of we

00:29:18.506 --> 00:29:20.425
thought Zuri's bedroom
would look like.

00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:22.761
As you can see, there's
a lot of pink around.

00:29:22.761 --> 00:29:24.304
And one thing that
we talked about

00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:27.140
is just like when we
were working on this,

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:31.478
it's 2017, 2018, 2019,
and we don't necessarily

00:29:31.478 --> 00:29:34.481
have to lean into the
stereotypical color palette

00:29:34.481 --> 00:29:36.274
that young girls are into.

00:29:36.274 --> 00:29:40.445
We can kind of push it and
make it a little bit more

00:29:40.445 --> 00:29:41.321
non-binary.

00:29:41.321 --> 00:29:43.615
So if you go to
the next slide, you

00:29:43.615 --> 00:29:46.242
can see that we ended up going
with the blue wall instead

00:29:46.242 --> 00:29:49.287
of all pink everything,
kind of having more pink

00:29:49.287 --> 00:29:54.334
and purple accents and really,
coming with the brown hardwood

00:29:54.334 --> 00:29:56.461
floors to give it
a lot of charm.

00:29:56.461 --> 00:30:00.089
And then the last
one is the hospital.

00:30:00.089 --> 00:30:02.675
And when we got this
design, we were just

00:30:02.675 --> 00:30:04.010
I don't think we had any notes.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:05.595
It just looked so good.

00:30:05.595 --> 00:30:07.847
He almost forgot we
needed the hospital.

00:30:07.847 --> 00:30:08.515
That I remember.

00:30:08.515 --> 00:30:09.265
It was like, wait.

00:30:09.265 --> 00:30:10.975
We forgot to design
the hospital.

00:30:10.975 --> 00:30:13.228
I guess we need to do that.

00:30:13.228 --> 00:30:14.062
Yeah.

00:30:14.062 --> 00:30:17.774
Again, just it's so beautiful,
and a majority of this

00:30:17.774 --> 00:30:19.067
was just more like location.

00:30:19.067 --> 00:30:20.860
Like, OK, she's going
to be at the window.

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:24.614
All right, we need to move the
TV and everything else and just

00:30:24.614 --> 00:30:25.990
making sure this
looked authentic

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:29.452
and again, Maria did
an incredible job.

00:30:29.452 --> 00:30:30.119
It's incredible.

00:30:30.119 --> 00:30:33.206
I'd love to talk about how
you developed the story.

00:30:33.206 --> 00:30:36.125
You have 6 and 1/2 minutes
to tell these three people's

00:30:36.125 --> 00:30:39.003
lives plus the cat.

00:30:39.003 --> 00:30:42.674
And let's talk a bit
about that journey.

00:30:42.674 --> 00:30:45.385
I'd worked a lot of
music videos and a lot

00:30:45.385 --> 00:30:47.053
of short-form filmmaking.

00:30:47.053 --> 00:30:50.932
And I always loved trying
to figure out twist endings

00:30:50.932 --> 00:30:54.686
and things that will shock
the system a little bit,

00:30:54.686 --> 00:30:56.479
but are also additive
to the narrative.

00:30:56.479 --> 00:30:59.774
And for me, the
idea of them going

00:30:59.774 --> 00:31:02.485
through this whole journey
of trying to do her hair,

00:31:02.485 --> 00:31:04.028
but then you see the reason.

00:31:04.028 --> 00:31:08.992
And the reason is because Mom
has lost confidence in herself

00:31:08.992 --> 00:31:10.743
and thinking that
she's maybe not

00:31:10.743 --> 00:31:13.454
as beautiful as she once was
because she's lost her hair.

00:31:13.454 --> 00:31:16.207
And the idea that Zuri
was able to get her dad

00:31:16.207 --> 00:31:18.084
to do her hair in a
style that her mom used

00:31:18.084 --> 00:31:22.213
to do, it's so
encompassing and just

00:31:22.213 --> 00:31:25.466
adds on a nice little
dimension and also showcases

00:31:25.466 --> 00:31:28.970
that they're a nuclear family
and kind of teasing the idea

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:30.471
that maybe he's a single dad.

00:31:30.471 --> 00:31:31.681
What happened to the mom?

00:31:31.681 --> 00:31:34.142
You kind of introduce all these
questions in the beginning,

00:31:34.142 --> 00:31:36.436
and now, I think people
really appreciated the fact

00:31:36.436 --> 00:31:39.063
that Mom was alive at the end.

00:31:39.063 --> 00:31:42.191
And then also, it went
even up until the credits,

00:31:42.191 --> 00:31:43.610
the different end
credits that we

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:46.779
had that showed mom's hair
growing back during each slide.

00:31:46.779 --> 00:31:49.657
Just things like that
that just showcase OK,

00:31:49.657 --> 00:31:53.620
not only is she going home,
but now she's getting better,

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:56.122
and they're going to
actually have a happy ending.

00:31:56.122 --> 00:31:58.541
So yeah, we can get into
a little bit of the story

00:31:58.541 --> 00:31:59.876
development.

00:31:59.876 --> 00:32:02.462
Go to the next slide.

00:32:02.462 --> 00:32:05.548
So in animation, and this is
my first experience with this,

00:32:05.548 --> 00:32:10.887
I mean, so much of it is just
like redoing and watching

00:32:10.887 --> 00:32:12.430
amongst a collective
group of people,

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.307
and all right, let's
all give notes on it.

00:32:14.307 --> 00:32:15.975
What's working,
what isn't, and just

00:32:15.975 --> 00:32:18.645
constantly, you're refining and
making sure that it's right.

00:32:18.645 --> 00:32:21.439
And I love that compared to
live action filmmaking, which

00:32:21.439 --> 00:32:23.399
I have a lot of experience in.

00:32:23.399 --> 00:32:26.778
And a lot of times being a
filmmaker is very isolating,

00:32:26.778 --> 00:32:29.030
and you're feeling like
a lot of the decisions

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:32.075
are on your shoulders and
it kind of lives and dies

00:32:32.075 --> 00:32:33.076
with the director.

00:32:33.076 --> 00:32:34.869
But the thing I
love about animation

00:32:34.869 --> 00:32:38.247
is you may not have the
best idea, and that's OK.

00:32:38.247 --> 00:32:42.585
You can take from whoever,
and if it makes sense,

00:32:42.585 --> 00:32:45.505
you add it to the final project,
and it's very collaborative.

00:32:45.505 --> 00:32:49.884
And so this was an original
ending that we had conceived.

00:32:49.884 --> 00:32:51.803
This is probably one of
the earlier beginnings

00:32:51.803 --> 00:32:53.096
of the short film that we had.

00:32:53.096 --> 00:32:55.348
And go ahead and play it,
and I'll explain to you

00:32:55.348 --> 00:32:56.557
why we changed it.

00:32:56.557 --> 00:32:59.560
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:33:27.922 --> 00:33:29.340
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:29.841
All right.

00:33:29.841 --> 00:33:32.176
So ignore the creepy
guy in the background.

00:33:32.176 --> 00:33:37.807
He came with the
design of the set.

00:33:37.807 --> 00:33:39.767
So this version of
the beginning was

00:33:39.767 --> 00:33:42.061
like we were trying to get
a lot of information across.

00:33:42.061 --> 00:33:44.397
We knew that the short film
wasn't going to have words,

00:33:44.397 --> 00:33:46.524
so we were going to have
to really show a lot.

00:33:46.524 --> 00:33:50.194
And the idea of this
was, Dad is exhausted.

00:33:50.194 --> 00:33:52.280
He's been on his own for
a couple of weeks, trying

00:33:52.280 --> 00:33:53.740
to take care of
Zuri and also, he's

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:55.575
trying to work on his
music because he's

00:33:55.575 --> 00:33:59.746
a music producer and also,
really wanting to showcase

00:33:59.746 --> 00:34:01.330
how independent Zuri is.

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:03.499
She's able to make breakfast.

00:34:03.499 --> 00:34:06.002
She's able to pick
up after herself.

00:34:06.002 --> 00:34:08.379
And she's trying
to sneak by her dad

00:34:08.379 --> 00:34:11.299
to grab his iPad so she can
get into these hair designs.

00:34:11.299 --> 00:34:13.926
And the thing that we
notice is like, OK,

00:34:13.926 --> 00:34:15.803
Dad just looks like
a horrible parent.

00:34:15.803 --> 00:34:16.554
He looks lazy.

00:34:16.554 --> 00:34:18.473
He looks like he's
just knocked out.

00:34:18.473 --> 00:34:20.725
We're not really able to
set up the reason why.

00:34:20.725 --> 00:34:23.853
And so we just we
agreed that Dad

00:34:23.853 --> 00:34:26.064
needed to be more
active in order to get

00:34:26.064 --> 00:34:27.523
that same information across.

00:34:27.523 --> 00:34:29.859
And then if you go
to the next one,

00:34:29.859 --> 00:34:31.903
we could show you the
2.0 version of that,

00:34:31.903 --> 00:34:34.739
trying to show Dad being
a little bit more active.

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:37.200
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:34:47.043 --> 00:34:49.962
- [SNIFFING]

00:34:59.305 --> 00:35:01.641
[END PLAYBACK]

00:35:01.641 --> 00:35:04.977
So this version of it shows
Dad being way more active.

00:35:04.977 --> 00:35:07.355
It definitely gets across the
fact that he's overwhelmed.

00:35:07.355 --> 00:35:09.524
He's trying to pick
up after himself.

00:35:09.524 --> 00:35:10.858
Food is burning on the stove.

00:35:10.858 --> 00:35:12.860
He's tripping over stuff.

00:35:12.860 --> 00:35:15.029
And the thing that we
found is that, obviously,

00:35:15.029 --> 00:35:17.490
with a short film, you want to
keep it as tight as possible,

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:21.661
and we felt that there was a
way to essentially introduce him

00:35:21.661 --> 00:35:24.205
later in the story
just to kind of set up

00:35:24.205 --> 00:35:27.542
Zuri's journey of being
very much an active kid,

00:35:27.542 --> 00:35:30.628
being confident in herself,
being able to get herself ready

00:35:30.628 --> 00:35:32.296
and to get into the bathroom.

00:35:32.296 --> 00:35:35.633
And we decided that we could
bring in Dad a little later

00:35:35.633 --> 00:35:37.844
in the story and introduce
him in that moment

00:35:37.844 --> 00:35:40.972
that you saw in the final short
film, where he's walking across

00:35:40.972 --> 00:35:43.307
with the laundry
basket and drops it

00:35:43.307 --> 00:35:47.645
and is immediately dropped
into the story in that way.

00:35:47.645 --> 00:35:51.566
And so the next clip that
you'll see that we ended up

00:35:51.566 --> 00:35:54.110
cutting out, which is the
last bit that we'll show you

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:57.280
in terms of some of the early
story stuff that didn't make it

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:00.241
was this idea of really
trying to set up Zuri.

00:36:00.241 --> 00:36:04.078
We realized that we wanted
Zuri to be very active.

00:36:04.078 --> 00:36:05.872
One of the biggest
questions with animation

00:36:05.872 --> 00:36:08.457
is, why are we animating it?

00:36:08.457 --> 00:36:11.460
This is live action.

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:14.088
You need to add things in
there that really justifies

00:36:14.088 --> 00:36:15.840
the use of the
medium, and one thing

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:19.510
that we realized that we wanted
was these fantasy sequences.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:24.390
And the initial idea was Zuri
having a big imagination, which

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:27.560
she's swiping through
the hairstyles,

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.313
she imagines herself with
these different hairstyles

00:36:30.313 --> 00:36:32.815
of which he may be doing in the
world with those hairstyles.

00:36:32.815 --> 00:36:35.985
And so let's play it, and I'll
break it down a little more.

00:36:35.985 --> 00:36:38.446
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:49.832
- [CROWD APPLAUSE]

00:36:58.716 --> 00:37:01.177
- [SCREAMING]

00:37:08.601 --> 00:37:19.445
- (SINGING)

00:37:19.445 --> 00:37:21.364
[END PLAYBACK]

00:37:21.364 --> 00:37:21.864
Right.

00:37:21.864 --> 00:37:24.992
And so obviously, those
scenes are very cute.

00:37:24.992 --> 00:37:27.411
The big thing for us though
that ended up happening

00:37:27.411 --> 00:37:31.290
was totally the fact that we
were living in Zuri's fantasies

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:34.877
for as long as we were felt like
they were stepping on the more

00:37:34.877 --> 00:37:37.838
serious ending of the short
film, where we were going

00:37:37.838 --> 00:37:40.716
to be dealing with,
obviously, Mom being sick,

00:37:40.716 --> 00:37:42.802
and we didn't really want
to step on that as much.

00:37:42.802 --> 00:37:45.263
And we came to the decision
that it would be best

00:37:45.263 --> 00:37:48.808
to quickly get through
Zuri trying to figure out

00:37:48.808 --> 00:37:51.310
the different hairstyles and
also, make the cat a little bit

00:37:51.310 --> 00:37:53.229
more active in her
decision making.

00:37:53.229 --> 00:37:55.856
And then in terms of
the fantasies, actually

00:37:55.856 --> 00:37:57.900
giving that to dad
and having that

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:01.028
be a way that he can
express like his fears

00:38:01.028 --> 00:38:03.781
in terms of this daunting task
of trying to do his daughter's

00:38:03.781 --> 00:38:07.618
hair and that not stepping
on the tone of the ending

00:38:07.618 --> 00:38:11.330
as much as us living in
Zuri's fantasies for so long.

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:14.583
So those are some deleted
scenes from the short film

00:38:14.583 --> 00:38:18.212
that exclusive group of
you have been able to see.

00:38:18.212 --> 00:38:21.549
And Karen will bring you through
some of the animation process.

00:38:21.549 --> 00:38:23.759
Yeah, and that's really
the secret sauce.

00:38:23.759 --> 00:38:27.096
If there's one thing that is
the reason why I love animation

00:38:27.096 --> 00:38:31.100
so much is that ability to just
write and rewrite and learn

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:34.228
by seeing it up
many, many times.

00:38:34.228 --> 00:38:39.525
I think that when we're
really kicking hard on this,

00:38:39.525 --> 00:38:41.360
it was probably a
year-long process,

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:44.530
but the first seven or I think
the first nine months were

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:47.658
really about just
rewriting these storyboards

00:38:47.658 --> 00:38:49.160
and making sure
that they were right

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:52.038
and getting everybody's
notes to get it right.

00:38:52.038 --> 00:38:54.206
Yeah, so then the next
stage is animation,

00:38:54.206 --> 00:38:55.958
and this was really
important to us

00:38:55.958 --> 00:38:58.210
and again, why we really
needed Bruce Smith to come in,

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:02.298
because we knew we really
wanted these characters to move

00:39:02.298 --> 00:39:03.799
as beautifully as possible.

00:39:03.799 --> 00:39:05.760
And none of us had
work in Harmony before.

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:07.928
We knew it mostly
from television shows,

00:39:07.928 --> 00:39:11.182
and again, wanted to just
make it as fluid as possible.

00:39:11.182 --> 00:39:15.394
So we were able to figure out
a process by which we could

00:39:15.394 --> 00:39:16.729
get the best of both worlds.

00:39:16.729 --> 00:39:20.566
So we wanted to search
for great 2D animators,

00:39:20.566 --> 00:39:22.526
hand-drawn animators
that could really give us

00:39:22.526 --> 00:39:24.403
the fluidity of what we wanted.

00:39:24.403 --> 00:39:27.239
And then they could put it
into the system called Harmony.

00:39:27.239 --> 00:39:28.949
And what Harmony
does is it builds

00:39:28.949 --> 00:39:31.202
a rig or a model
for the characters,

00:39:31.202 --> 00:39:34.830
and you're able to actually
take the 2D animation that you

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:38.626
do first and apply it to
the rig so the rig can

00:39:38.626 --> 00:39:40.336
follow the movements
of the 2D animators.

00:39:40.336 --> 00:39:42.004
So the first couple
I want to show you

00:39:42.004 --> 00:39:44.298
is an amazing
animator, Mike Owens.

00:39:44.298 --> 00:39:49.428
And you'll see how fluid and
cartoony you can make it,

00:39:49.428 --> 00:39:51.305
and then we'll show
you what the final is.

00:39:51.305 --> 00:39:53.724
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:39:58.604 --> 00:40:02.274
- So when you do 2D, you can see
the squash and stretch and just

00:40:02.274 --> 00:40:04.652
sort of the personality
that can come out.

00:40:04.652 --> 00:40:09.532
So we really loved what
Mike was bringing there.

00:40:09.532 --> 00:40:11.617
And it allowed him to
really find the character

00:40:11.617 --> 00:40:14.120
without working in the model,
and he didn't know that system.

00:40:14.120 --> 00:40:16.163
So we were able to do that.

00:40:16.163 --> 00:40:18.499
And then if you move to,
I believe the next one,

00:40:18.499 --> 00:40:20.709
you can see what the
final looks like.

00:40:25.297 --> 00:40:27.550
So you get some of that
performance in the storyboards,

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:29.135
but really, it came
to life in animation.

00:40:29.135 --> 00:40:29.718
[END PLAYBACK]

00:40:29.718 --> 00:40:33.222
That's really so
beautiful and so worthy

00:40:33.222 --> 00:40:36.225
of the ambition
you all had for it

00:40:36.225 --> 00:40:38.644
and just an incredible
piece of art.

00:40:38.644 --> 00:40:40.396
So congratulations on that.

00:40:40.396 --> 00:40:41.730
We're going to go to questions.

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:43.649
So everyone is going
to get teed up.

00:40:43.649 --> 00:40:45.776
In the meantime, I'm
going to start off

00:40:45.776 --> 00:40:50.614
with this question that's
more Academy Gold focus.

00:40:50.614 --> 00:40:52.032
So many of our
students are really

00:40:52.032 --> 00:40:53.534
thinking about
working on pitching

00:40:53.534 --> 00:40:57.580
their ideas as a project
for the Gold program.

00:40:57.580 --> 00:41:00.833
I'm curious as to when
you went in the room

00:41:00.833 --> 00:41:04.336
to pitch this, in
your pitch sessions,

00:41:04.336 --> 00:41:07.089
a few questions in that regard.

00:41:07.089 --> 00:41:11.677
Who was involved in that first
pitch in terms of your team?

00:41:11.677 --> 00:41:13.554
Who was in the room
from the other side?

00:41:13.554 --> 00:41:15.764
And did you go through
the whole deck in the way

00:41:15.764 --> 00:41:19.351
you did for us today, or
what did that look like?

00:41:19.351 --> 00:41:21.770
So who, on your side,
who on their side,

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:25.107
what did you show them,
and what happened?

00:41:25.107 --> 00:41:27.860
I love that you think
that we pitched this.

00:41:30.654 --> 00:41:33.532
Kickstarter was our pitch.

00:41:33.532 --> 00:41:34.950
We went straight to the people.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:37.620
I think that's
something that I think

00:41:37.620 --> 00:41:40.998
is so amazing about the world
today with social media.

00:41:40.998 --> 00:41:43.250
If you build up your
following enough

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:45.920
or if you have enough people
that will support your vision,

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:48.339
or if you've proven that
you're the type of person that

00:41:48.339 --> 00:41:52.092
will follow through on things,
you can take it to the internet

00:41:52.092 --> 00:41:53.302
and crowdfund.

00:41:53.302 --> 00:41:56.680
And sometimes,
strange things happen.

00:41:56.680 --> 00:41:58.390
Sometimes you just never know.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:01.602
You may be dropping a
project at the perfect time,

00:42:01.602 --> 00:42:05.064
and the right person retweets
it, it now goes viral,

00:42:05.064 --> 00:42:08.484
and now it's on studio's radars.

00:42:08.484 --> 00:42:12.446
The thing for us is that I
knew for a fact that if I were

00:42:12.446 --> 00:42:19.161
to went to Disney, Pixar, maybe
even Sony without having proven

00:42:19.161 --> 00:42:21.956
anything had been like, hey, I
want to do a short film about

00:42:21.956 --> 00:42:24.166
a Black dad who's doing
his daughter's hair,

00:42:24.166 --> 00:42:26.168
I probably would have got
laughed out the room--

00:42:26.168 --> 00:42:27.336
you know what I mean--

00:42:27.336 --> 00:42:28.337
in a way.

00:42:28.337 --> 00:42:33.801
And the reason I say that is
also because 9 times out of 10,

00:42:33.801 --> 00:42:35.511
it's also about
the executives that

00:42:35.511 --> 00:42:36.929
are on the other
side of the table

00:42:36.929 --> 00:42:40.224
and people that are able to
relate to these experiences.

00:42:40.224 --> 00:42:43.978
Sony is great in that they
have people like Karen that

00:42:43.978 --> 00:42:45.396
are actually
decision makers that

00:42:45.396 --> 00:42:47.690
are able to see
the humanity and I

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:50.359
think in stories that are a
little broader and a little bit

00:42:50.359 --> 00:42:51.443
more inclusive.

00:42:51.443 --> 00:42:54.363
That's not the case with
a lot of other studios.

00:42:54.363 --> 00:42:57.491
And I think that in terms of
being a creative nowadays,

00:42:57.491 --> 00:43:00.869
you just have to bet on
yourself and do the work

00:43:00.869 --> 00:43:08.043
and be that a short film,
be that a presentation

00:43:08.043 --> 00:43:09.420
or just trying to
raise the money

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:11.755
and kind of bringing the
attention and the momentum

00:43:11.755 --> 00:43:12.631
to you.

00:43:12.631 --> 00:43:13.716
That's what we did.

00:43:13.716 --> 00:43:16.885
So I'll say Kickstarter
was our pitch pages

00:43:16.885 --> 00:43:21.473
and having raised $300,000 and
going viral, that just gave us

00:43:21.473 --> 00:43:24.310
so much more momentum to be
able to have conversations

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:25.644
with people like Kerry.

00:43:25.644 --> 00:43:27.438
And just to jump in,
I mean, you're right.

00:43:27.438 --> 00:43:30.899
Sony doesn't have
a shorts program,

00:43:30.899 --> 00:43:33.819
so my involvement was,
as an outside producer,

00:43:33.819 --> 00:43:36.030
doing it just on the
love of the project

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:37.823
until it started
to gain momentum

00:43:37.823 --> 00:43:40.367
and we could actually
show what we were doing.

00:43:40.367 --> 00:43:43.579
And then Sony got on board and
realized how important it was,

00:43:43.579 --> 00:43:45.414
but Matthew is exactly right.

00:43:45.414 --> 00:43:46.832
Going to the people
and the people

00:43:46.832 --> 00:43:50.669
proving that there's a need
made my job much easier.

00:43:50.669 --> 00:43:53.213
I couldn't have done
it without that.

00:43:53.213 --> 00:43:54.923
The funny thing,
though, is we did,

00:43:54.923 --> 00:43:58.177
as you know we're
doing a television show

00:43:58.177 --> 00:43:59.887
version of this.

00:43:59.887 --> 00:44:01.972
The thing about that
was the first time

00:44:01.972 --> 00:44:06.143
I'd ever been on the other
side pitching to a network.

00:44:06.143 --> 00:44:07.936
I'm like, wait a minute,
I'm on this side,

00:44:07.936 --> 00:44:10.648
so that was the scary part.

00:44:10.648 --> 00:44:15.819
But it didn't hurt having
an Academy win behind it.

00:44:15.819 --> 00:44:17.446
Did you bring the
statue with you

00:44:17.446 --> 00:44:19.782
and just put him on the table
and say, make this film.

00:44:19.782 --> 00:44:20.824
That was the whole pitch.

00:44:20.824 --> 00:44:22.201
That was the pitch.

00:44:22.201 --> 00:44:23.786
How you like me now.

00:44:23.786 --> 00:44:26.664
To be fair, they
knew it was valuable

00:44:26.664 --> 00:44:29.875
before we won the
Oscar, so props to HBO

00:44:29.875 --> 00:44:32.711
for really seeing that beauty.

00:44:32.711 --> 00:44:33.212
Fantastic.

00:44:33.212 --> 00:44:35.464
So our Academy Gold
folks are here with us,

00:44:35.464 --> 00:44:38.550
and we're going to start
out with our questions.

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:40.177
Destiny, you're up first.

00:44:40.177 --> 00:44:40.678
Hello.

00:44:40.678 --> 00:44:41.178
Hi.

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:41.887
How are you?

00:44:41.887 --> 00:44:44.848
Thank you for coming.

00:44:44.848 --> 00:44:47.184
I wanted to ask one
question because my biggest

00:44:47.184 --> 00:44:51.271
fear as a Black creator is
that my Black mind will have

00:44:51.271 --> 00:44:56.026
to be compromised for coming
off socially or politically

00:44:56.026 --> 00:44:56.527
correct.

00:44:56.527 --> 00:44:59.697
So I wanted to know
as Black creators,

00:44:59.697 --> 00:45:02.074
have you ever felt
through this process

00:45:02.074 --> 00:45:03.951
that you have to
compromise yourself,

00:45:03.951 --> 00:45:08.706
and how did you go about
that and stand your ground?

00:45:08.706 --> 00:45:11.375
You know what's so interesting
is that I've always

00:45:11.375 --> 00:45:14.920
been pretty outspoken,
especially when things aren't

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:15.421
right.

00:45:15.421 --> 00:45:21.260
And for the most part,
it's worked for me.

00:45:21.260 --> 00:45:24.888
I've yet to have a
seat here, and let

00:45:24.888 --> 00:45:27.474
me show you your Twitter
timeline and kind of where

00:45:27.474 --> 00:45:29.518
you're messing up.

00:45:29.518 --> 00:45:32.354
But I think that's something
that everybody struggles with.

00:45:32.354 --> 00:45:34.481
You want to be your
authentic self.

00:45:34.481 --> 00:45:36.024
I do think that
there is pressure

00:45:36.024 --> 00:45:37.651
to compromise yourself.

00:45:37.651 --> 00:45:40.195
You may be trying to sell a
project and people are like,

00:45:40.195 --> 00:45:43.115
well, you may maybe tone this
down or tone that down there

00:45:43.115 --> 00:45:43.949
maybe we'll buy it

00:45:43.949 --> 00:45:46.285
And I think you just
have to be in a position

00:45:46.285 --> 00:45:49.371
where you're able to
make that decision.

00:45:49.371 --> 00:45:52.040
Is it worth compromising
that, and if it isn't, know

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:53.542
that everything
isn't for everybody.

00:45:53.542 --> 00:45:58.213
Maybe it's not destined for
you to work with that network.

00:45:58.213 --> 00:46:02.217
There was a story that came
out about Michaela Coel, who

00:46:02.217 --> 00:46:06.263
has that new series out
on HBO, I May Destroy You

00:46:06.263 --> 00:46:08.307
and how she went to Netflix.

00:46:08.307 --> 00:46:10.768
They were trying to get a
certain level of ownership,

00:46:10.768 --> 00:46:13.228
and she said, no,
and she stepped away,

00:46:13.228 --> 00:46:14.271
and she knew her value.

00:46:14.271 --> 00:46:17.357
And then HBO stepped
in, and I don't

00:46:17.357 --> 00:46:18.734
know exactly what
her terms were,

00:46:18.734 --> 00:46:21.779
but they had to be more amenable
to what she wanted to do,

00:46:21.779 --> 00:46:23.655
and she ended up
going with them.

00:46:23.655 --> 00:46:26.033
And so I think it's just
situations like that.

00:46:26.033 --> 00:46:28.368
The more that you
work in this industry,

00:46:28.368 --> 00:46:29.787
I think the more
leverage you have

00:46:29.787 --> 00:46:35.083
to get your unflinching,
unapologetic vision out there.

00:46:35.083 --> 00:46:36.710
But when you are
starting out, you

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:38.170
may have to make
some compromises,

00:46:38.170 --> 00:46:40.964
and I think it's just a matter
of are those things that you're

00:46:40.964 --> 00:46:43.050
willing to live
with to get ahead

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:45.594
or to try to get those first
couple opportunities or not.

00:46:45.594 --> 00:46:48.305
And don't feel like
you have to do that.

00:46:48.305 --> 00:46:51.058
You can maybe do something to
take it straight to the people

00:46:51.058 --> 00:46:51.975
and prove it that way.

00:46:51.975 --> 00:46:54.978
And then you could be like
this is a proven entity

00:46:54.978 --> 00:46:57.773
that people will support,
and take it or leave it.

00:46:57.773 --> 00:47:01.026
Ashley, you're up next.

00:47:01.026 --> 00:47:02.027
Hi, Matthew and Karen.

00:47:02.027 --> 00:47:04.279
It's really great
to meet both of you.

00:47:04.279 --> 00:47:07.407
This film means so
much to me, personally.

00:47:07.407 --> 00:47:08.992
It captured so much
of my childhood

00:47:08.992 --> 00:47:12.079
so perfectly on screen, so it
was really exciting to see.

00:47:12.079 --> 00:47:14.373
And I want to thank you both
for showing us the concept

00:47:14.373 --> 00:47:15.958
art and clips, and
I hope that they'll

00:47:15.958 --> 00:47:17.793
make their way into the
actual series on HBO

00:47:17.793 --> 00:47:20.087
too because they looked
really, really cool, especially

00:47:20.087 --> 00:47:21.004
the superhero concept.

00:47:21.004 --> 00:47:22.297
Oh, for sure.

00:47:22.297 --> 00:47:25.384
And so for both of you, my
question is really general,

00:47:25.384 --> 00:47:28.178
but if you have any advice
for aspiring Black writers

00:47:28.178 --> 00:47:30.806
or producers who are trying
to enter the industry,

00:47:30.806 --> 00:47:31.849
I'd love to hear it.

00:47:31.849 --> 00:47:34.309
And from both of you, are
there any other genres

00:47:34.309 --> 00:47:35.811
that you're interested
in exploring,

00:47:35.811 --> 00:47:38.564
whether it's animation
or otherwise?

00:47:38.564 --> 00:47:42.526
You want to go first, Karen?

00:47:42.526 --> 00:47:43.485
I'm excited for you.

00:47:43.485 --> 00:47:46.196
I remember just trying
to start your career.

00:47:46.196 --> 00:47:47.906
It's scary, but it's
also really exciting

00:47:47.906 --> 00:47:50.325
to just get going and just
all the possibilities.

00:47:50.325 --> 00:47:53.537
And I just think that
when I talk to writers,

00:47:53.537 --> 00:47:56.540
and you know this, that it has
to be something, obviously,

00:47:56.540 --> 00:47:58.500
that you can't not
do because you're

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:00.752
going to face so many
obstacles that you just

00:48:00.752 --> 00:48:03.046
have to be so passionate that
there's just nothing else

00:48:03.046 --> 00:48:04.631
that you could imagine
yourself doing.

00:48:04.631 --> 00:48:07.676
And just keep doing it, and
keep finding every opportunity

00:48:07.676 --> 00:48:09.052
to kind of hone your craft.

00:48:09.052 --> 00:48:13.265
And when you say genres,
I love that because I

00:48:13.265 --> 00:48:15.392
think it's really exciting,
especially in animation

00:48:15.392 --> 00:48:17.603
for us to explore genres
that have never really

00:48:17.603 --> 00:48:18.562
been done before.

00:48:18.562 --> 00:48:21.815
So even Spider-Verse, because it
was sort of a more all audience

00:48:21.815 --> 00:48:24.693
and it felt like a more
live action Marvel movie

00:48:24.693 --> 00:48:26.945
in good ways, but then it
was things you could do only

00:48:26.945 --> 00:48:29.156
in animation, I
think the way we keep

00:48:29.156 --> 00:48:32.034
pushing the envelope with all
genres are going to be great.

00:48:32.034 --> 00:48:35.037
I'm excited about doing horror
in animation, all of that.

00:48:35.037 --> 00:48:36.997
And I think that
more, as a writer,

00:48:36.997 --> 00:48:39.458
that you are able to
try different things,

00:48:39.458 --> 00:48:41.168
the more you're going
to hone your craft.

00:48:41.168 --> 00:48:43.629
I can't watch horror,
really, but horror

00:48:43.629 --> 00:48:47.591
takes you through every beat
and manipulates the audience

00:48:47.591 --> 00:48:50.719
like no other, like comedy,
but in a different kind of way.

00:48:50.719 --> 00:48:54.097
And so I think the more you
really just stretch yourself,

00:48:54.097 --> 00:48:56.767
even if you've never
tried it before,

00:48:56.767 --> 00:48:59.853
it'll all kind of distill
down to do something great.

00:48:59.853 --> 00:49:04.650
And just back to, say for
for Matthew and Hair Love,

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:07.069
it's like there were so many
dimensions of what he wanted

00:49:07.069 --> 00:49:08.654
to say and now that
I think that's why

00:49:08.654 --> 00:49:10.364
this short was very rich.

00:49:10.364 --> 00:49:12.366
So I think that when
you're thinking of writing,

00:49:12.366 --> 00:49:15.035
it's like you can be
very layered in it.

00:49:15.035 --> 00:49:17.496
The ones that don't work for
me are the ones that are like,

00:49:17.496 --> 00:49:20.666
oh, it's just, it's a Black
astronaut, or it's like

00:49:20.666 --> 00:49:21.249
or this that.

00:49:21.249 --> 00:49:22.668
And it's like, OK,
yeah, but what?

00:49:22.668 --> 00:49:23.293
We get you.

00:49:23.293 --> 00:49:24.753
And we get that in
the first scene,

00:49:24.753 --> 00:49:27.881
but how are you really
taking it into another level.

00:49:27.881 --> 00:49:30.634
So just continue
to push yourself.

00:49:30.634 --> 00:49:32.344
Yeah, I would agree.

00:49:32.344 --> 00:49:34.930
I mean, with Hair Love, there
were so many levels to it.

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:38.100
It was like wanting to see more
representation and animation,

00:49:38.100 --> 00:49:40.852
wanting young Black boys
and girls to see themselves

00:49:40.852 --> 00:49:43.730
in animation, to see people
wearing their hair like that.

00:49:43.730 --> 00:49:45.399
And then like, oh,
this is the character

00:49:45.399 --> 00:49:47.401
that I can take
pride in, and I want

00:49:47.401 --> 00:49:49.111
to have their poster
in my room, and I

00:49:49.111 --> 00:49:52.155
want to wear their pin and
the backpack and all of that.

00:49:52.155 --> 00:49:57.536
And then also, again,
even with our ending,

00:49:57.536 --> 00:50:00.664
also wanting to bring
attention to hair loss

00:50:00.664 --> 00:50:03.250
and how that's being rampant
in the Black community

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:06.586
and how alopecia and
other issues lead to that

00:50:06.586 --> 00:50:09.756
and how that leads to a
lack of self-confidence.

00:50:09.756 --> 00:50:11.299
So just really
trying to touch it

00:50:11.299 --> 00:50:13.468
on a lot of different levels.

00:50:13.468 --> 00:50:17.305
My advice would be writing
is writing and rewriting.

00:50:17.305 --> 00:50:18.015
Writers write.

00:50:18.015 --> 00:50:18.932
Directors direct.

00:50:18.932 --> 00:50:20.308
Producers produce.

00:50:20.308 --> 00:50:22.894
If you want to be a writer, you
have to constantly be writing,

00:50:22.894 --> 00:50:25.647
and you have to have
more than one idea.

00:50:25.647 --> 00:50:27.899
Because you may go and
pitch that one idea,

00:50:27.899 --> 00:50:30.193
and it may be good enough
to get you on the radar,

00:50:30.193 --> 00:50:32.404
but they may not want to
make it, but they're like,

00:50:32.404 --> 00:50:33.155
what else you got?

00:50:33.155 --> 00:50:35.282
And you want to be
able to-- while you

00:50:35.282 --> 00:50:37.492
have people's
attention-- to come at it

00:50:37.492 --> 00:50:38.785
from a lot of different angles.

00:50:38.785 --> 00:50:41.288
And your first
couple of projects

00:50:41.288 --> 00:50:43.957
may not be the thing that
gets your foot in the door.

00:50:43.957 --> 00:50:46.209
I've been working in
Hollywood for 13 years.

00:50:46.209 --> 00:50:50.589
I've done 30 plus music videos,
two live action short films,

00:50:50.589 --> 00:50:53.675
two live action feature films,
10 episodes of TV directing.

00:50:53.675 --> 00:50:56.219
And it took an animated
short film for me

00:50:56.219 --> 00:50:57.679
to get my foot
fully in the door.

00:50:57.679 --> 00:51:00.849
You know what I mean, in a
genre I never worked in before.

00:51:00.849 --> 00:51:04.478
So you just gotta keep
at it, and I truly

00:51:04.478 --> 00:51:09.399
believe that it takes
typically, five years or so

00:51:09.399 --> 00:51:11.860
for a lot of people to really
get that first break and then

00:51:11.860 --> 00:51:14.821
about 10 for them
to really make it

00:51:14.821 --> 00:51:17.783
and be a force in the industry.

00:51:17.783 --> 00:51:20.827
Think of it like that and
constantly be working,

00:51:20.827 --> 00:51:22.746
and you'll be fine.

00:51:22.746 --> 00:51:23.914
Thank you so much.

00:51:23.914 --> 00:51:24.414
Sure.

00:51:24.414 --> 00:51:27.042
Alejandra, you're up next.

00:51:27.042 --> 00:51:27.793
Hello, everyone.

00:51:27.793 --> 00:51:29.127
Thank you so much for your time.

00:51:29.127 --> 00:51:31.421
This presentation
has been amazing.

00:51:31.421 --> 00:51:33.632
My question is,
well, I would like

00:51:33.632 --> 00:51:38.053
to know more about your
whole Kickstarter campaign.

00:51:38.053 --> 00:51:38.929
How did it work?

00:51:38.929 --> 00:51:41.389
What else do you do.

00:51:41.389 --> 00:51:44.184
What do you think it helped
you achieve your goal,

00:51:44.184 --> 00:51:47.521
and not even achieve it,
just surpassed it by far,

00:51:47.521 --> 00:51:49.564
besides the amazing
idea, of course.

00:51:49.564 --> 00:51:51.233
But I don't know.

00:51:51.233 --> 00:51:52.150
Did you send emails?

00:51:52.150 --> 00:51:56.947
Did you text people, your
incentives, and all that stuff.

00:51:56.947 --> 00:51:58.657
I mean, I'll be very
transparent with it.

00:51:58.657 --> 00:52:00.450
This was my third
Kickstarter campaign.

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:03.245
Typically, what I do, I've done
them for both my live action

00:52:03.245 --> 00:52:03.745
features.

00:52:03.745 --> 00:52:05.705
And typically, I would
launch a campaign

00:52:05.705 --> 00:52:08.792
after I shot the project, and
then it'd be like, all right,

00:52:08.792 --> 00:52:10.585
we're about to premiere
South by Southwest.

00:52:10.585 --> 00:52:12.838
And then the week that
announcement goes out,

00:52:12.838 --> 00:52:14.339
then I put the
campaign out to try

00:52:14.339 --> 00:52:18.718
to raise money for finishing
funds and things like that.

00:52:18.718 --> 00:52:20.095
This was different
because it was

00:52:20.095 --> 00:52:23.932
like I had nothing to
show, say for some artwork.

00:52:23.932 --> 00:52:25.767
I didn't have a trailer
to drop or anything.

00:52:25.767 --> 00:52:27.811
Literally, it was just
a video, me being like,

00:52:27.811 --> 00:52:31.565
this is the idea like a
car salesman or whatever.

00:52:31.565 --> 00:52:34.985
And the reason why I feel
like this Kickstarter was

00:52:34.985 --> 00:52:37.320
the most successful
on top of the idea

00:52:37.320 --> 00:52:39.948
was, I put a lot more
effort into building up

00:52:39.948 --> 00:52:42.617
my social media before
the campaign launched.

00:52:45.996 --> 00:52:49.332
In 2016, I maybe had
25,000 followers.

00:52:49.332 --> 00:52:52.711
And when Trump got, elected
I think for everybody,

00:52:52.711 --> 00:52:55.839
for me, it made me be like, OK,
if you're not saying anything,

00:52:55.839 --> 00:52:56.631
you're complicit.

00:52:56.631 --> 00:52:58.133
So you've got to
be way more active.

00:52:58.133 --> 00:53:00.886
You can't be scared
to be divisive.

00:53:00.886 --> 00:53:03.096
You've got to call things
out when they're wrong.

00:53:03.096 --> 00:53:05.724
And when Trump got
elected, I just

00:53:05.724 --> 00:53:08.101
found myself way
more active in terms

00:53:08.101 --> 00:53:10.562
of not even about film
stuff, but just like, OK,

00:53:10.562 --> 00:53:12.606
the cages is a right.

00:53:12.606 --> 00:53:14.191
Typical things that
you would think

00:53:14.191 --> 00:53:16.526
are just like common knowledge,
but for whatever reason,

00:53:16.526 --> 00:53:18.361
they end up being
divisive on social media,

00:53:18.361 --> 00:53:20.822
and I found my following grow.

00:53:20.822 --> 00:53:23.909
And I think a combination of
talking about political stuff,

00:53:23.909 --> 00:53:26.620
talking about myself,
showing pictures of my dogs,

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:29.664
just like people getting
to know you as a person,

00:53:29.664 --> 00:53:32.667
I was able to build it up
to around 100,000 followers

00:53:32.667 --> 00:53:34.878
when the Kickstarter
campaign launched.

00:53:34.878 --> 00:53:37.797
And typically, when you're
doing a campaign like this,

00:53:37.797 --> 00:53:40.592
typically, maybe
2% or 3% of people

00:53:40.592 --> 00:53:44.012
that follow you will actually
pull out their wallet and type

00:53:44.012 --> 00:53:45.513
their credit card
information, you

00:53:45.513 --> 00:53:47.057
know what I mean,
to support you.

00:53:47.057 --> 00:53:48.850
And you've got to think
about it like that.

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:50.936
And the more people that
follow you in that way

00:53:50.936 --> 00:53:53.563
and know you personally
and know your journey

00:53:53.563 --> 00:53:55.273
are the more people
that will follow you.

00:53:55.273 --> 00:53:57.817
And I think it was a
combination of that

00:53:57.817 --> 00:54:00.904
plus people like
Lin Manuel Miranda,

00:54:00.904 --> 00:54:02.906
maybe not donating, but
sharing their campaign

00:54:02.906 --> 00:54:04.699
to their 3 million followers.

00:54:04.699 --> 00:54:06.618
Chelsea Clinton,
Ben Stiller, I mean,

00:54:06.618 --> 00:54:09.746
so many people that, and also
being intentional with that,

00:54:09.746 --> 00:54:13.416
being like, OK, is you
donating $20 more valuable,

00:54:13.416 --> 00:54:16.503
or are you retweeting this to
your millions of followers?

00:54:16.503 --> 00:54:19.839
We had really cool gifts like
one of the more popular gifts

00:54:19.839 --> 00:54:21.716
that I've always
offered on my campaigns

00:54:21.716 --> 00:54:24.094
was the idea of having
your name in the credits,

00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:27.264
if you donated $50 or $60.

00:54:27.264 --> 00:54:30.267
I never thought this thing
would be as big as it was.

00:54:30.267 --> 00:54:37.107
I mean, on 9 Rides, and Last
Fall, we maybe had 100 names.

00:54:37.107 --> 00:54:41.069
With Hair Love, we had 1,300
names in our in credits.

00:54:41.069 --> 00:54:44.030
And I never would
have anticipated

00:54:44.030 --> 00:54:47.409
that many people got
involved, and that was always

00:54:47.409 --> 00:54:49.536
the more popular one.

00:54:49.536 --> 00:54:51.454
And just being creative
you, depending on what

00:54:51.454 --> 00:54:52.247
your project is.

00:54:52.247 --> 00:54:53.707
For us, a book made sense.

00:54:53.707 --> 00:54:55.583
T-shirts made sense.

00:54:55.583 --> 00:54:58.920
Pens made sense, but that
may be different for you

00:54:58.920 --> 00:54:59.879
and your project.

00:54:59.879 --> 00:55:03.300
So just being very custom and
thinking about what people like

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:05.051
and also, not doing
too many things that

00:55:05.051 --> 00:55:08.722
are too expensive because
then the creation of the gifts

00:55:08.722 --> 00:55:10.932
and shipping them out take
away from your final money

00:55:10.932 --> 00:55:11.725
that you raised to.

00:55:11.725 --> 00:55:13.893
So I hope that was helpful.

00:55:13.893 --> 00:55:16.354
One thing I want to say
is, from a studio side,

00:55:16.354 --> 00:55:18.898
not one penny was
spent in marketing.

00:55:18.898 --> 00:55:23.361
So it's so hard to get
people's attention,

00:55:23.361 --> 00:55:24.946
just to pay attention
to anything.

00:55:24.946 --> 00:55:27.574
So even if you're not
doing a Kickstarter,

00:55:27.574 --> 00:55:30.618
let's say and you're doing a
studio movie, studios value

00:55:30.618 --> 00:55:33.204
filmmakers that have a
connection to their audience

00:55:33.204 --> 00:55:35.332
because that just makes it
that much easier to find

00:55:35.332 --> 00:55:39.502
an authentic way
in to an audience.

00:55:39.502 --> 00:55:40.628
Thank you so much.

00:55:40.628 --> 00:55:41.129
All right.

00:55:41.129 --> 00:55:42.589
That does it for our questions.

00:55:42.589 --> 00:55:44.966
I want to thank you
all for the questions,

00:55:44.966 --> 00:55:49.179
but also, particularly, Matthew
H Cherry, Karen Rupert Toliver.

00:55:49.179 --> 00:55:53.099
If we didn't before, we
all have Hair Love now.

00:55:53.099 --> 00:55:55.810
We're so happy to be
a part of the family,

00:55:55.810 --> 00:55:57.771
if just for this afternoon.

00:55:57.771 --> 00:56:00.440
So on behalf of Academy
Gold, I thank you,

00:56:00.440 --> 00:56:02.942
and I personally thank you
for this delightful experience

00:56:02.942 --> 00:56:06.279
and for the gift of your film
and the projects to come.

00:56:06.279 --> 00:56:09.532
So congratulations,
and thank you.

